@tmds, thinking about it more, I think we should remove the synchronization from the Unix implementation rather than adding it to the Windows one.  I expect it'll be the minority case that someone needs to remove such a handler, and even more rare that the code following that removal depends on the handler not running, and in the case where it does, the code itself can synchronize if really necessary.

On top of that, it's not hard to come up with cases where this could deadlock, e.g. one thread takes a lock and does Console.CancelKeyPress -= handler concurrently with their handler running and trying to take that same lock... locking as the Unix implementation does now could cause that to deadlock.

Do you want to remove it?  Maybe also simplify the scheme employed to match the Windows one at the same time?

